WEBVTT
TITLE Memory Hierarchy And Cache — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A memory hierarchy works because programs repeat themselves: the same address is used again soon, and its neighbours are used soon after.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Each level is bigger, slower and cheaper per byte than the one above, and holds copies of blocks taken from the level below.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
A hit costs one access at that level. A miss costs that access plus a trip to the next level, which is the miss penalty.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Temporal locality is the same address twice. Spatial locality is a neighbour. A block is what turns spatial locality into hits.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Average memory access time is the hit time plus the miss rate times the miss penalty: T equals T-hit plus m times P.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
GATE 2015 Set 2: 80 per cent hits at 5 ns with a 50 ns miss gives 0.8 times 5 plus 0.2 times 50, which is 14 ns.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Hierarchical access adds the cache time to the penalty. Simultaneous access looks in both at once, so the miss costs only the lower level time.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
So read what the paper hands you: hit time and penalty, or hit time and full miss time. The formula follows the words.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Every address splits into three fields: the offset picks the byte inside the block, the index picks the set, and the tag is what is left.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Offset bits are log base 2 of the block size, index bits log base 2 of the number of sets, and the three must add to the address width.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
GATE 2021 Set 1: a 32-bit byte address, a 32 KB direct-mapped cache, 64-byte blocks. Offset 6, index 9, tag 17.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
GATE 2025 Set 1 asked for the total tag storage: 1024 lines times 6 bits, which is 6144 bits, not 6.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Direct mapped: block b may live only in line b modulo L, so there is exactly one place to look and no choice to make.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Set associative: block b goes to set b modulo v, and may sit in any of that set's k lines.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Fully associative: one set, so a block may sit anywhere, and there are no index bits at all.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
GATE 2013 asked for the line range of block j: from j modulo v times k up to that plus k minus 1.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Raising associativity to k divides the number of sets, so the index loses log base 2 of k bits and the tag gains exactly those bits.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
GATE 2017 Set 1: a 10-bit tag in a direct-mapped cache becomes 14 bits at 16-way, because 16 is 2 to the 4.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
GATE 2021 Set 2 runs the exchange backwards: 2 KB of cache, 64-byte blocks, a 22-bit tag in a 32-bit address, so 4 index bits, 16 sets and 2 ways.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Associativity buys fewer conflict misses and pays with a wider tag and a slower comparison.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
On a miss into a full set, a replacement policy names the victim. Least recently used names the line unused for the longest time.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
GATE 2004: two-way with four blocks, so two sets of two lines. Every block in the trace is even, so all five accesses land in set 0.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
The walk: 8, 12 and 0 each miss and fill, 12 hits, then 8 misses again and evicts 0. Four misses in all.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
The first three misses are unavoidable. The last is not: the same trace in four fully associative lines misses only three times.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
A compulsory miss is the first reference to a block. No cache size and no associativity removes it.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
A conflict miss disappears when the same capacity is made fully associative, because nothing is then forced to share a set.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
A capacity miss survives full associativity and goes away only when the cache is larger than the working set.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
So classify by counterfactual: re-run the same trace in another cache and see which misses vanish. The page does exactly that.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
A write hit always updates the cache. The policy decides whether main memory is updated now or later.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Write-through writes memory on every store, so no dirty bit is needed and eviction moves no data.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Write-back writes only the cache and sets the dirty bit. The block goes to memory when it is evicted.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
GATE 2022: the false statements are A, B and D. Only C, that evicting from a write-through cache moves no data, is true.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
With two levels the outcomes are exclusive: L1 hit, or L1 miss with L2 hit, or both miss.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
GATE 2025 Set 1: 0.95 times 10 plus 0.0425 times 20 plus 0.0075 times 200, which is 11.85 ns.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
For the processor, stall cycles per instruction are the accesses per instruction times the miss rate times the miss penalty.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
That is why cache questions end as CPI or speedup questions: the memory system changes the cycles, not the instruction count.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Step one: split the address into offset, index and tag, and check that the three widths add to the address width.

38
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.000
Step two: name the mapping, because it fixes the number of sets and therefore the index width.

39
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Step three: if a trace is given, count hits, misses and evictions, not addresses.

40
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.000
Step four: use the access-time convention the paper used, and convert units once, at the end.

41
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Step five: for a speedup, divide times, never miss rates. And carry the clock if the question changed it.

